{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"\nLet me analyze this task carefully.\n\nThe task SE-PE-01 requires implementing a `solve(case)` function that takes a payload with:\n- `nodes`: a list of tree nodes, each with `id`, `parent`, `symbol`, `depth`, `removable`, `utility`\n- `required_symbols`: a list of symbols that must be present\n\nThe output should be:\n- `removed_node_ids`: list of node IDs that were removed\n- `retained_node_ids`: list of node IDs that were retained\n\nLet me figure out the logic by analyzing the examples:\n\n**Case 0:**\nNodes:\n- root: parent=null, symbol=\"root\", removable=false, utility=1.0\n- n1: parent=\"root\", symbol=\"s1\", removable=false, utility=0.87578184\n- n2: parent=\"root\", symbol=\"s2\", removable=true, utility=0.54663808\n- n3: parent=\"root\", symbol=\"s0\", removable=false, utility=0.43607337\n- n4: parent=\"n2\", symbol=\"s1\", removable=true, utility=0.66557814\n- n5: parent=\"n3\", symbol=\"s2\", removable=false, utility=0.70145921\n- n6: parent=\"n1\", symbol=\"s0\", removable=true, utility=0.19506725\n\nrequired_symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n\nThe tree structure:\n- root\n  - n1 (s1, not removable)\n    - n6 (s0, removable)\n  - n2 (s2, removable)\n    - n4 (s1, removable)\n  - n3 (s0, not removable)\n    - n5 (s2, not removable)\n\nrequired_symbols are \"root\" and \"s1\". This means we need to keep at least one node with symbol \"root\" and at least one with symbol \"s1\".\n\nThe nodes that are not removable: root, n1, n3, n5\nThe nodes that are removable: n2, n4, n6\n\nSince n1 has symbol \"s1\" and is not removable, that requirement is automatically satisfied.\nSince root has symbol \"root\" and is not removable, that requirement is automatically satisfied.\n\nNow, which removable nodes can be removed?\n\nFor a node to be removed, it must be removable AND its removal must not cause any required symbol to disappear from the tree (considering ancestors must exist for a node to count).\n\nWait, actually I need to think about this differently. The problem is about a tree pruning where:\n1. Non-removable nodes cannot be removed\n2. When a node is removed, all its descendants are also removed (subtree pruning)\n3. We need to keep at least one node for each required symbol\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The task is about which nodes get removed. Key constraints:\n- `removable=false` nodes cannot be removed\n- If a node is removed, its children are also removed (tree structure)\n- The required_symbols must still be covered by retained nodes\n\nThe goal seems to be to remove as many nodes as possible (maximizing removed set) while keeping all required symbols covered.\n\nWait, but what about utility? Let me think again...\n\nActually, looking at the structure again - perhaps the goal is to prune the tree by removing subtrees (where the root of the subtree is removable) while ensuring:\n1. Required symbols are still covered by at least one retained node\n2. Non-removable nodes are never removed\n\nBut what's the optimization objective? Maybe it's to remove removable nodes/subtrees while maintaining coverage of required symbols.\n\nLet me think about what makes sense:\n- For case 0: required_symbols = [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n- \"root\" is covered by the root node (non-removable, always kept)\n- \"s1\" is covered by n1 (non-removable) and n4 (removable, child of n2)\n- Since n1 is non-removable and covers \"s1\", we can safely remove n2 (and its subtree including n4)\n- n6 is removable and covers \"s0\" (not required), so it can be removed\n- Result would be: removed=[n2, n4, n6], retained=[root, n1, n3, n5]\n\nFor case 1: required_symbols = [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n- Same tree structure, same symbols\n- n1 (s1, non-removable) covers \"s1\"\n- n6 is removable, symbol=\"s0\" (not required)\n- n2 is removable, symbol=\"s2\" (not required)\n- n4 is child of n2, symbol=\"s1\" - but n1 already covers \"s1\"\n- Result would be: removed=[n2, n4, n6], retained=[root, n1, n3, n5]\n\nFor case 2: Same analysis, same result.\n\nSo the logic seems to be:\n1. A node can be removed if it's removable AND removing it (and its subtree) doesn't eliminate coverage of any required symbol\n2. When we remove a node, its entire subtree is removed\n3. We want to remove all removable nodes that can safely be removed\n\nActually, I need to be more careful. Let's say we're looking at a removable node. If we remove it, we also remove all its descendants. So the question is: does removing this subtree cause any required symbol to become uncovered?\n\nA symbol is \"covered\" if there exists at least one retained node with that symbol.\n\nSo the algorithm would be:\n1. For each removable node (processed in some order), determine if removing it along with its subtree would leave all required symbols still covered.\n2. Remove the node if it can be safely removed.\n\nBut the order matters! We should probably process top-down (remove higher nodes first, since they take out entire subtrees).\n\nActually, the greedy approach: try to remove each removable subtree root, in order from root downward. If a subtree can be removed (i.e., all required symbols are still present in remaining nodes), remove it.\n\nLet me verify with case 0:\n- Start with all nodes: {root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, n6}\n- Process depth 1 removable nodes: n2 (removable, depth=1)\n  - Subtree of n2: {n2, n4}\n  - Remaining without {n2, n4}: {root, n1, n3, n5, n6}\n  - Symbols covered: {root, s1, s0, s2} - all required symbols (root, s1) are covered\n  - Remove n2 and n4\n- Process depth 1 non-removable: n1, n3 - skip\n- Process depth 2 removable: n6 (removable, depth=2)\n  - Subtree of n6: {n6}\n  - Remaining without {n6}: {root, n1, n3, n5}\n  - Symbols covered: {root, s1, s0, s2} - all required symbols covered\n  - Remove n6\n- Result: removed={n2, n4, n6}, retained={root, n1, n3, n5}\n\nThis matches my earlier analysis.\n\nNow let me think about the algorithm more carefully:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    \n    # Build node lookup and children map\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['parent'] is not None:\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\n    \n    # Get subtree of a node (including the node itself)\n    def get_subtree(node_id):\n        result = set()\n        stack = [node_id]\n        while stack:\n            curr = stack.pop()\n            result.add(curr)\n            stack.extend(children.get(curr, []))\n        return result\n    \n    # Current set of retained nodes\n    retained = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\n    \n    # Process nodes in BFS order (depth order)\n    from collections import deque\n    queue = deque()\n    # Find root\n    root_id = next(n['id'] for n in nodes if n['parent'] is None)\n    queue.append(root_id)\n    \n    while queue:\n        node_id = queue.popleft()\n        node = node_map[node_id]\n        \n        if node_id not in retained:\n            # Already removed (as part of a subtree)\n            continue\n        \n        if node['removable']:\n            # Try removing this subtree\n            subtree = get_subtree(node_id)\n            remaining = retained - subtree\n            \n            # Check if all required symbols are still covered\n            covered = set(node_map[nid]['symbol'] for nid in remaining)\n            if required_symbols.issubset(covered):\n                # Safe to remove\n                retained -= subtree\n                continue  # Don't add children to queue since they're removed\n        \n        # Add children to queue\n        for child_id in children.get(node_id, []):\n            if child_id in retained:\n                queue.append(child_id)\n    \n    removed = set(n['id'] for n in nodes) - retained\n    \n    return {\n        'removed_node_ids': list(removed),\n        'retained_node_ids': list(retained)\n    }\n```\n\nWait, but I need to think about whether the order matters for maximizing removals.\n\nConsider a case where:\n- A is a removable node with required symbol S\n- B is a child of A, removable, without required symbols\n- There's another node C (not in A's subtree) that also has symbol S\n\nIf we process A first: we check if removing A's subtree leaves S covered (yes, via C). So we remove A and B.\nIf we process B first: B can be removed. Then process A: A doesn't have S in its subtree anymore (B is gone), and C covers S, so A can be removed too.\n\nBoth orderings give the same result in this case. \n\nBut what if:\n- A is removable with symbol S (required)\n- A has children: B (removable, no required symbols)\n- C is another node with symbol S (non-removable)\n\nProcessing A first: subtree={A,B}, remaining has C with symbol S. Remove A and B.\nProcessing B first: remove B. Then process A: subtree={A}, remaining has C with symbol S. Remove A.\n\nSame result.\n\nWhat about this: \n- A is removable, symbol S (required)\n- A has child B, removable, symbol T (required)\n- Both S and T are elsewhere in the tree\n\nIf we process A first: remove {A, B} - both required symbols still covered elsewhere. Good.\nIf we process B first: B can be removed (T covered elsewhere). Then A: remove A (S covered elsewhere).\n\nSame result.\n\nI think the BFS top-down greedy works well because:\n1. If a subtree root can be removed, removing it early is always at least as good as removing individual nodes later\n2. Non-removable nodes act as blockers\n\nActually, let me reconsider. What if the greedy top-down approach prevents removals that would otherwise be possible?\n\nHmm, actually I think the point is to greedily prune subtrees. If a removable subtree can be pruned without losing coverage, prune it. Otherwise, recurse into children.\n\nLet me code this up properly:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    \n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        children[n['id']] = []\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['parent'] is not None:\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\n    \n    def get_subtree_ids(node_id):\n        result = []\n        stack = [node_id]\n        while stack:\n            curr = stack.pop()\n            result.append(curr)\n            stack.extend(children.get(curr, []))\n        return result\n    \n    retained = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\n    \n    from collections import deque\n    root_id = next(n['id'] for n in nodes if n['parent'] is None)\n    queue = deque([root_id])\n    \n    while queue:\n        node_id = queue.popleft()\n        \n        if node_id not in retained:\n            continue\n        \n        node = node_map[node_id]\n        \n        if node['removable']:\n            subtree = set(get_subtree_ids(node_id))\n            remaining = retained - subtree\n            covered_symbols = {node_map[nid]['symbol'] for nid in remaining}\n            \n            if required_symbols.issubset(covered_symbols):\n                retained -= subtree\n                continue\n        \n        for child_id in children.get(node_id, []):\n            queue.append(child_id)\n    \n    all_ids = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\n    removed = all_ids - retained\n    \n    return {\n        'removed_node_ids': sorted(list(removed)),\n        'retained_node_ids': sorted(list(retained))\n    }\n```\n\nShould I sort the output? The schema just says arrays of strings, no ordering requirement. I'll keep sorted for consistency.\n\nActually, wait - should the output be sorted? The schema doesn't specify ordering. Let me just keep the order natural or use sorted for determinism.\n\nLet me verify the logic once more with Case 0:\n\nInitial retained: {root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, n6}\nQueue: [root]\n\nProcess root:\n- Not removable\n- Add children: n1, n2, n3\n\nQueue: [n1, n2, n3]\n\nProcess n1:\n- Not removable\n- Add children: n6\n\nQueue: [n2, n3, n6]\n\nProcess n2:\n- Removable!\n- Subtree of n2: {n2, n4}\n- Remaining: {root, n1, n3, n5, n6}\n- Covered symbols: {root, s1, s0, s2} ⊇ {root, s1} ✓\n- Remove {n2, n4}, retained = {root, n1, n3, n5, n6}\n\nQueue: [n3, n6] (n2 removed, n4 won't be processed)\n\nProcess n3:\n- Not removable\n- Add children: n5\n\nQueue: [n6, n5]\n\nProcess n6:\n- Removable!\n- Subtree of n6: {n6}\n- Remaining: {root, n1, n3, n5}\n- Covered symbols: {root, s1, s0, s2} ⊇ {root, s1} ✓\n- Remove {n6}, retained = {root, n1, n3, n5}\n\nQueue: [n5]\n\nProcess n5:\n- Not removable\n- No children\n\nFinal: retained = {root, n1, n3, n5}, removed = {n2, n4, n6}\n\nThis looks correct! Now let me write the final implementation.\n\nOne thing to consider: the problem says the function must not mutate `case`. My implementation doesn't mutate `case`, it creates new data structures.\n\nAlso, I should handle the case where a node's children might not be in the retained set when we add them to the queue. But I have a check `if node_id not in retained: continue` at the start, which handles nodes that were removed as part of a subtree.\n\nActually wait - when I process n2 and remove its subtree, n4 would still be in the queue if it were added. But since I process n2 before adding its children to the queue (because I `continue` when removing), n4 never gets added. However, if n4 were already in the queue for some reason, the `if node_id not in retained: continue` check would handle it.\n\nI think the implementation is correct. Let me finalize it.\n\nThe response needs to be a JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a string.\n\n```json\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    \\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is not None:\\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\\n    \\n    def get_subtree_ids(node_id):\\n        result = []\\n        stack = [node_id]\\n        while stack:\\n            curr = stack.pop()\\n            result.append(curr)\\n            result_children = children.get(curr, [])\\n            stack.extend(result_children)\\n        return result\\n    \\n    retained = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\\n    \\n    from collections import deque\\n    root_id = next(n['id'] for n in nodes if n['parent'] is None)\\n    queue = deque([root_id])\\n    \\n    while queue:\\n        node_id = queue.popleft()\\n        \\n        if node_id not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        \\n        node = node_map[node_id]\\n        \\n        if node['removable']:\\n            subtree = set(get_subtree_ids(node_id))\\n            remaining = retained - subtree\\n            covered_symbols = {node_map[nid]['symbol'] for nid in remaining}\\n            \\n            if required_symbols.issubset(covered_symbols):\\n                retained -= subtree\\n                continue\\n        \\n        for child_id in children.get(node_id, []):\\n            queue.append(child_id)\\n    \\n    all_ids = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\\n    removed = all_ids - retained\\n    \\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': sorted(list(removed)),\\n        'retained_node_ids': sorted(list(retained))\\n    }\"}\n```\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is not None:\\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\\n\\n    def get_subtree_ids(node_id):\\n        result = []\\n        stack = [node_id]\\n        while stack:\\n            curr = stack.pop()\\n            result.append(curr)\\n            stack.extend(children.get(curr, []))\\n        return result\\n\\n    retained = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\\n\\n    from collections import deque\\n    root_id = next(n['id'] for n in nodes if n['parent'] is None)\\n    queue = deque([root_id])\\n\\n    while queue:\\n        node_id = queue.popleft()\\n\\n        if node_id not in retained:\\n            continue\\n\\n        node = node_map[node_id]\\n\\n        if node['removable']:\\n            subtree = set(get_subtree_ids(node_id))\\n            remaining = retained - subtree\\n            covered_symbols = {node_map[nid]['symbol'] for nid in remaining}\\n            if required_symbols.issubset(covered_symbols):\\n                retained -= subtree\\n                continue\\n\\n        for child_id in children.get(node_id, []):\\n            queue.append(child_id)\\n\\n    all_ids = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\\n    removed = all_ids - retained\\n\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': sorted(list(removed)),\\n        'retained_node_ids': sorted(list(retained))\\n    }\"}","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_01IgSmIruZ57S50TgDnyP5hi","model":"claude-opus-4-7","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2217},"cache_creation_input_tokens":2217,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":60,"output_tokens":4769,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":4332},"service_tier":"standard"}}